Proving a Negation The rules of Section 2.1 use a negation: ulename{
eg E} records a clash, and ulename{ ot E} turns a clash into any statement we like. Neither rule can prove a negation unless the lines already clash: ulename{ ot E} writes one only from a line  ot. So we still cannot prove that no rational number has square 2, or even that x^2 + 1 is never 0. This section adds the one rule that proves a negation, ulename{
eg I}, and uses it for one of the oldest proofs in mathematics. How to prove that something is not so To show that no rational number has square 2, we cannot inspect the rational numbers one by one: there are infinitely many. A mathematician instead supposes the opposite and shows that it leads to something impossible: “suppose r is rational and r^2 = 2; …; this is a contradiction; hence r^2 
e 2.” Try the smaller goal x^2 + 1 
e 0, for a real number x, with the rules so far. The goal is 
eg(x^2 + 1 = 0), and its main connective is 
eg. Chapter 1’s goal-directed move asks for the introduction rule of the main connective, and 
eg has none yet. The rules of Section 2.1 do not help: ulename{ ot E} could write the goal, but only from a line  ot, and nothing available clashes. The statement x^2 + 1 = 0 is not a line of anything; it is the thing we want to rule out. The mathematician’s sentence shows the missing rule. “Suppose r^2 = 2” opens a box, exactly like the box of ulename{{	o}I} in @def-rules-implication, and “this is a contradiction” is its last line,  ot. The only new thing is what the box gives when it closes. To prove “not \varphi”, assume \varphi and reach a contradiction. [Rules for Negation] The left schema is ulename{
eg I} (not-introduction): the assumption \varphi is discharged when the box closes, and the line  ot is a line of that box itself, not of a box inside it. The right schema is ulename{
eg E}, recalled from @def-rules-contradiction, so that the rule that proves a negation and the rule that uses one stand together. This rule has conditions that matter; read each one. 1. Open a box whose first line is the assumption \varphi. It is the unnegated statement: the thing we want to refute, not the thing we want to prove. Any statement may be assumed. In a finished derivation this assumption is printed “asm, for ulename{
eg I}”: the annotation names the rule that will close the box, as “asm, for ulename{{	o}I}” did in Section 1.4. 2. Inside the box every rule may be used, and every line available just above the box may be cited, as in any box (@def-derivation). 3. The last line of the box is exactly  ot, and it is a line of that box itself, not inside a further box opened within it. This is the same wording as for ulename{{	o}I}, and every box rule shares it. 4. Close the box and write 
eg\varphi on the next line, outside it. The justification cites the box as a range, “ulename{
eg I} i–j”. The assumption is discharged: after the box closes, nothing inside it may be cited, and only the negation leaves. 5. The new line is always a negation. ulename{
eg I} never writes a statement whose main connective is not 
eg. The degenerate case is a box of one line, whose assumption is already  ot. The box \lines{1}{1} then ends on  ot as soon as it opens, and ulename{
eg I} gives 
eg ot: So \vdash 
eg ot: “it is not the case that contradiction” is derivable from nothing, as it should be. A noninstance by one change. Take a box opened by \varphi whose last line is 
eg\varphi instead of  ot. It looks as if the assumption has refuted itself, and ulename{
eg I} still does not apply: the box must end on  ot. One more line,  ot by ulename{
eg E} citing the assumption and the line 
eg\varphi, repairs it. The same holds if  ot is reached only inside a smaller box within the box: it is not then a line of the box itself. A box that never uses its assumption. A box may reach  ot without ever using its assumption. That is legal, but it is a sign: if no line of the box depends on \varphi, the same lines could have been written just above the box, where  ot would then stand, and ulename{ ot E} would have written 
eg\varphi, or anything else, directly. The lines available above the box were already contradictory. When your ulename{
eg I} box never uses its assumption, look for the error above the box, not in it. Why this rule. The statement 
eg\varphi says that \varphi fails, and the box shows what would happen if \varphi held: together with the lines above it, it would lead to  ot, which never holds. So in any situation where the lines above the box hold, \varphi cannot hold. That is exactly what 
eg\varphi claims. The rules for 
eg have the same shapes as rules we already know, with  ot in one slot. Put them beside the rules for 	o, applied to the statement \varphi 	o  ot: ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- To … a negation 
eg\varphi an implication \varphi 	o  ot ----------------------- -------------------------------------------------- -------------------------------------------------- prove it box from \varphi to  ot, then ulename{
eg I} box from \varphi to  ot, then ulename{{	o}I} use it, given \varphi  ot by ulename{
eg E}  ot by ulename{{	o}E} ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- Row by row, 
eg\varphi behaves exactly like \varphi 	o  ot: “not \varphi” means “\varphi leads to contradiction”. Some presentations of logic take this as the definition of 
eg. This book keeps 
eg as a connective with its own rules. Do not open the box with what you want to prove. To prove 
eg\varphi, the box opens with \varphi. A box opened by 
eg\varphi and ending in  ot gives 
eg
eg\varphi by ulename{
eg I}, the negation of the goal, not the goal. So a box opened with 
eg(x > 0) and closed by ulename{
eg I} gives 
eg
eg(x > 0), not x > 0; Section 2.5 shows the other kind of “suppose not”. This is the negation’s version of the converse error that Section 1.4 found in Argument B. In the Givens/Goal table, ulename{
eg I} moves \varphi into the Givens and makes  ot the goal. The first example: a number that is never zero The goal we could not reach at the start of the section comes first. [A negative statement proved by not-introduction] Let x be a real number. Prove that x^2 + 1 
e 0, naming the rules. The goal x^2 + 1 
e 0 is 
eg(x^2 + 1 = 0), a negation, so open a box on x^2 + 1 = 0 and look for a clash. The school fact x^2 \ge 0, that is 
eg(x^2 < 0), is the negation available; its unnegated half x^2 < 0 is the subgoal, and algebra on the assumption produces it. The goal x^2 + 1 
e 0 is 
eg(x^2 + 1 = 0), so we use ulename{
eg I}. Suppose x^2 + 1 = 0 [	ext{assumption for } ulename{
eg I}]. Then x^2 = -1, so x^2 < 0. But x^2 \ge 0, that is, 
eg(x^2 < 0), so we have a contradiction [ulename{
eg E}]. Hence x^2 + 1 
e 0 [ulename{
eg I}]. With the school fact as the premise: In this derivation, which box does ulename{
eg I} cite, and which statement opened it? The box \lines{2}{4}, opened by x^2 + 1 = 0, the unnegated statement. The conclusion on line 5 is its negation, x^2 + 1 
e 0. First derivations with not-introduction The goal-directed move of this section: a goal 
