eg P is a negation, so assume P and aim at  ot; ulename{\vee I} turns P into P \vee Q, which clashes with the premise. The rules are ulename{\vee I}, ulename{
eg E}, and ulename{
eg I}. The box is the same as in @exm-manufactured-clash up to its line  ot. There the goal was P 	o R, so the box had to end on R, written by ulename{ ot E}, and closed by ulename{{	o}I}. Here the goal is 
eg P, so the box ends on  ot itself and closes by ulename{
eg I}. In words: if neither P nor Q holds, then in particular P does not. In the next derivation the clash itself creates a goal that is a negation, and the move of this section is used a second time, one box inside another. [Three negations become one] Show that 
