eg P), so the goal-directed move opens a second ulename{
eg I} box, on 
eg P, with goal  ot. Inside it, 
eg P clashes with the outer assumption P. This is the first derivation in which a ulename{
eg I} box is opened for a subgoal that a clash created. The outer box opened, its goal  ot at its foot (left); then the clash with the premise chosen, so that 
eg
eg P becomes the goal of the gap (right). Seven lines. Line 4 may cite line 2, because the only box containing line 2, the box \lines{2}{6}, also contains line 4 (@def-derivation). The inner box \lines{3}{4} ends on  ot as a line of its own, and so does the outer box \lines{2}{6}, at line 6; each ulename{
eg I} cites its own box. Notice what did not happen: no line removes a pair of negations. Three became one by adding them, twice, and clashing. How the rule looks in a mathematical proof The proof that no rational number has square 2 has the shape of ulename{
eg I}: the claim is negative, so we assume its opposite and look for  ot. The tool that finds the clash is @thm-square-even-implies-even, and the school fact it needs is about writing fractions: (L) Every rational number can be written as p/q with p and q integers, q 
e 0, and p and q not both even. This holds because a fraction whose numerator and denominator are both even can be reduced by 2, and the reduction cannot go on for ever, since the denominator shrinks in absolute value each time. Making “cannot go on for ever” precise needs a fact about the natural numbers that this book does not prove; here we take (L) as known. [Irrationality of the Square Root of Two] There is no rational number whose square is 2. So if a real number \sqrt{2} with square 2 exists, it is irrational. Fix a rational number r and prove the negative statement r^2 
e 2 by ulename{
eg I}: assume r^2 = 2 and hunt for  ot. Write r = p/q as in (L), so that the equation becomes p^2 = 2q^2. The only available negation is “not both even”, so the clash must come from showing that p and q are both even; @thm-square-even-implies-even is the tool for that, used twice. Before reading on: the second use of @thm-square-even-implies-even needs the line “q^2 is even”. From which two earlier lines of the proof will it come? Let r be a rational number, and suppose r^2 = 2 [	ext{assumption for } ulename{
eg I}]. By (L), r = p/q for integers p and q with q 
e 0, not both even. Then p^2 = r^2 q^2 = 2q^2, so p^2 is even. By @thm-square-even-implies-even, p is even [ulename{{	o}E}], say p = 2m for an integer m. Then 4m^2 = 2q^2, so q^2 = 2m^2, and q^2 is even. By @thm-square-even-implies-even again, q is even [ulename{{	o}E}]. So p and q are both even [ulename{\wedge I}], while they were chosen not both even; this is a contradiction [ulename{
eg E}]. Hence r^2 
e 2 [ulename{
eg I}]. Since r was an arbitrary rational number, no rational number has square 2. This proves the theorem. In the proof, which step is ulename{
eg E}, and which two statements does it cite? The step “this is a contradiction”. It cites “p and q are both even”, derived inside the box of r^2 = 2 by ulename{\wedge I}, and “p and q are not both even”, which (L) supplied when p and q were chosen. In the derivation below these are lines 11 and 2, and the step is line 12. The propositional part of the proof fits in a derivation. Fix r and the integers p, q that (L) supplies. Lines 1 and 2 record what (L) says about them; lines 3 and 4 are @thm-square-even-implies-even for n = p and n = q; the lines marked “algebra” are the computations of the proof. Line 13 is r^2 
e 2. The box \lines{5}{12} is a ulename{
eg I} box as the schema asks: opened by the unnegated statement r^2 = 2, and ending on  ot as a line of its own. Three steps of the proof lie outside the derivation: applying @thm-square-even-implies-even, a fact about every integer, to p and to q; choosing p, q and m; and passing from the arbitrary r to every rational number. All three are steps about “some” and “every”, used as ordinary reasoning (the note at the start of this chapter). Notice also what the proof does not need: that \sqrt{2} exists as a real number. The theorem is a statement about rational numbers only. And notice which rule closes it. “Suppose r^2 = 2” opens a box whose negation is the goal, so the proof is ulename{
eg I}, even though it is what everyone calls a proof by contradiction. The rule card Before the card, the rule at work on large statements. Each row is one use of ulename{
eg I}: the box, and the line it writes. [Instances of ulename{
eg I}] ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- \varphi The box New line ------------------ ------------------------------------- ------------------------------- P \vee 
