eg P is false. [Looking ahead] Sets and Functions, Chapter 3 proves (L) from a fact about the natural numbers called well-ordering. Analysis on the Real Line, Chapter 1 uses this section’s theorem in exactly the form proved here, as evidence that the rational numbers have a gap where \sqrt{2} should be. Exercises A. Check your understanding [A1] 1. Write the schema of ulename{
eg I} from memory, and say which statement opens its box. 2. Which rule does this sentence use, and which box does it close? “Suppose 0 had a reciprocal y. Then 1 = 0 \cdot y = 0, which is absurd. So 0 has no reciprocal.” 3. In the proof of @thm-sqrt2-irrational, the sentence “p is even” follows from “p^2 is even”. Which result and which rule justify it? 4. Where in the proof of @thm-sqrt2-irrational is (L) used, and what would go wrong if (L) said only “r = p/q with q 
e 0”? 5. Run the proof of @thm-sqrt2-irrational with 4 in place of 2: assume r^2 = 4 and write r = p/q as in (L). Which step can no longer be justified? (The conclusion “no rational number has square 4” is false, so some step must fail.) (a) The schema is the left one of @def-rules-negation: a box opened by the assumption \varphi, ending on  ot as a line of the box itself, then 
eg\varphi outside it, citing the box. The box opens with the unnegated statement \varphi. (b) ulename{
eg I}. The conclusion is the negative statement “0 has no reciprocal”; the box opens with “0 has a reciprocal y”, and “which is absurd” is its line  ot, reached by ulename{
eg E} from 1 = 0 and the fact 1 
