eg E} of line 12 cites. Without that clause the proof still derives “both even”, but it contradicts nothing (2 = 4/2 has both even), and no  ot is reached. (e) With r^2 = 4 we get p^2 = 4q^2 = 2(2q^2), so p^2 is even and p is even, p = 2k. Then 4k^2 = 4q^2, so q^2 = k^2. This is where the proof stalls: nothing says q^2 is even, so @thm-square-even-implies-even cannot be applied to q. Indeed for r = 2 = 2/1 we have q = 1, which is odd. Every step before the stall is correct; the proof simply fails to reach  ot. [A2: Drill] The parts run from one step, through a missing line, a justification column and steps to judge, to short derivations. 1. Which statement opens the ulename{
