eg P has reached the stage below. Name the move that opens each box, then complete it. The goal is an implication, so the outer box opens on Q, with goal 
eg P. That goal is a negation, so the inner box opens on P, with goal  ot. Inside it, the premise fires on P and gives 
eg Q, which clashes with the outer assumption Q. Line 5 may cite line 2 because the box \lines{2}{6} contains line 5. Both boxes were read off the goal: the outer one by Chapter 1’s move for an implication, the inner one by this section’s move for a negation. In words: if P rules out Q, then Q rules out P. [B2: A quadratic with no zero] Let x be a real number. Prove that x^2 - x + 1 
e 0, naming the rules. Hint: complete the square. The goal is 
eg(x^2 - x + 1 = 0), a negation, so we use the rule ulename{
eg I}. Suppose that x^2 - x + 1 = 0, as the assumption for ulename{
eg I}. Completing the square, x^2 - x + 1 = \left( x - 	frac12 ight)^2 + 	frac34 , so (x - 	frac12)^2 = -	frac34, and therefore (x - 	frac12)^2 < 0. But the square of a real number is never negative, that is, 
eg((x - 	frac12)^2 < 0); this is a contradiction [ulename{
eg E}]. Hence x^2 - x + 1 
e 0 [ulename{
eg I}]. This is the pattern of @exm-square-plus-one, with the square (x - 	frac12)^2 in place of x^2. The identity can be checked by expanding: (x - 	frac12)^2 = x^2 - x + 	frac14, and 	frac14 + 	frac34 = 1. [B3: The square root of three, used later] You may use that every integer can be written as 3k, 3k + 1 or 3k + 2 with k an integer, in one way only, and that every rational number can be written as p/q with integers p, q, q 
e 0, and p, q not both divisible by 3. 1. Prove that if n is an integer and n^2 is divisible by 3, then n is divisible by 3. 2. Hence prove that no rational number has square 3. (a) Suppose n^2 is divisible by 3 [	ext{assumption for } ulename{{	o}I}], so n^2 has the form 3j with j an integer. The integer n has the form 3k, 3k + 1 or 3k + 2, and we argue by cases [ulename{\vee E}]; a three-way split is two uses of ulename{\vee E}, one inside a box of the other. Case n = 3k. Then n is divisible by 3, as required. Cases n = 3k + 1 and n = 3k + 2. Then  egin{aligned} (3k + 1)^2 &= 9k^2 + 6k + 1 = 3(3k^2 + 2k) + 1, \ (3k + 2)^2 &= 9k^2 + 12k + 4 = 3(3k^2 + 4k + 1) + 1 , \end{aligned} so n^2 has the form 3j' + 1 as well as the form 3j. An integer has only one of the three forms, so this is a contradiction [ulename{
