Four Levels of One Proof Sections 8.1 and 8.2 matched each rule with the phrases that carry it. A proof, though, is read and written whole. This section writes proofs several times, from a full derivation to the plain prose of a textbook, names the four levels this gives, fixes exactly which steps the last level may leave unsaid, and ends with the use that matters most to a reader working alone: when a sentence looks doubtful, rewrite it, and only it, as a skeleton, and look. One short proof, four texts Here is one proof, for every integer n: if n is odd, then n^2 is odd, written four times, starting from the version a textbook prints and zooming in. Plain prose. Let n be odd, say n = 2k + 1. Then n^2 = 4k^2 + 4k + 1 = 2(2k^2 + 2k) + 1, so n^2 is odd. Prose with the rules tagged. Let n be an integer [ulename{ orall I}], and suppose n is odd [ulename{{	o}I}]; choose an integer k with n = 2k + 1 [ulename{\exists E}, fresh k]. Then n^2 = 4k^2 + 4k + 1 = 2(2k^2 + 2k) + 1 [algebra], so n^2 is odd, with the witness 2k^2 + 2k [ulename{\exists I}; then ulename{\exists E} closes, since “n^2 is odd” does not mention k]. Since n was arbitrary, every odd integer has an odd square [ulename{{	o}I}, ulename{ orall I}, closing]. The skeleton (domain 
Z). The full derivation. The skeleton again, with its line 3 split into two arith lines, each recording one fact: the square of 2k_0 + 1, then a factor 2 taken out. Four texts, one proof. Each keeps the box of n, the assumption that n is odd, the choice of k and the computation; they differ in how much of the routine work the reader is trusted to restore. A derivation, a skeleton and a paragraph are the same proof; they differ in what the reader is trusted to restore. In the plain prose, which words stand for line 2 of the skeleton, and which skeleton lines have no words at all? “Say n = 2k + 1” is line 2, the opening of the ulename{\exists E} box with the fresh name k. Lines 5, 6 and 7, the closings of the three boxes, have no words. “So n^2 is odd” is line 4, the ulename{\exists I}. Sections 2.2 and 6.4 already printed proofs twice, once as a derivation and once as a paragraph. The four texts you have just read are the four levels of the next definition. [Levels of Proof] A proof can be written at four levels. - Level 0, the full derivation. A derivation in the sense of @def-derivation, with the rules of Chapters 1, 2 and 5: every line is a premise, an assumption, or one rule applied to available lines. A derived rule used as one line (@def-derived-rule), such as the bounded rules of @prp-bounded-quantifier-rules, counts as one rule. Facts about numbers enter as premises, or as lines justified “arith”, each of which records one such fact applied to the lines it cites. - Level 1, the skeleton. The boxes and the logical lines only: every assumption, every flagged name, every line that opens or closes a box, with the rule that closes it, and every line that such a closing line cites. Each stretch of mathematics between them is compressed into one line, justified “arith”, “by def”, or by a cited result; a stretch of routine logical steps inside one box is compressed into one line justified by the names of its rules in order, such as “ulename{ orall E}, ulename{\exists I}”. - Level 2, annotated prose. Sentences, each tagged in brackets with the rule or rules it carries, such as [ulename{ orall I}] or [ulename{\exists E}]. - Level 3, plain prose. Sentences without tags, in which some steps are left unsaid. Which steps may be left unsaid is fixed by the convention that follows, not by the writer. In words: level 0 hides no logic. Every line other than an arith line can be checked mechanically against the schema of its rule, and each arith line records one fact about numbers that can be checked by hand. Level 1 keeps the logical architecture and lets the arithmetic run in blocks. Level 2 is level 1 turned into sentences, with the architecture still labeled. Level 3 removes the labels and some of the lines, following a fixed convention. The levels are already on the page in earlier chapters. The derivation in Section 6.4 is level 0. The paragraph after @thm-sqrt2-irrational, with its tags [ulename{{	o}E}], [ulename{\wedge I}], [ulename{
